=== FOURTH-PASS FIXES (apply these on top of all passes below) ===

--- Formation speed fine-tuning (wave 2 too slow to start) ---
Reported from actual play: wave 2's march read as too slow/too easy at the
start, but the speed it eventually escalates to after enough step-downs felt
right. Raising the START without changing anything else would also raise
that eventual ceiling proportionally (moveSpeed * speedRampPerEdge^N scales
with the start for the same N) - a maxMoveSpeed cap decouples the two.

@@ FormationController.cs, fields @@
-    [SerializeField] private        float moveSpeed        = 3f;
+    [SerializeField] private        float moveSpeed        = 4.5f; // raised again - 3 was still too slow to read as an immediate threat
+    [SerializeField] private        float maxMoveSpeed      = 7f;  // NEW - the ceiling moveSpeed can never escalate past
     [SerializeField] private        float speedRampPerEdge  = 1.2f;

@@ FormationController.cs, the hitEdge block at the end of Update() @@
         if (hitEdge)
         {
             direction  *= -1;
-            moveSpeed  *= speedRampPerEdge;
+            moveSpeed  = Mathf.Min(moveSpeed * speedRampPerEdge, maxMoveSpeed); // NEW - same ramp, but capped
         }

  IMPORTANT - Unity Inspector gotcha: changing a [SerializeField]'s default
  value in the .cs file does NOT retroactively update a component that
  already exists in your scene with the OLD value serialized. If your
  FormationController's Move Speed field in the Inspector still shows 1.5 or
  3 after applying this, that's why - type the new value in by hand, or
  click the component's gear icon (top-right) and choose Reset. This is the
  actual reason wave 2 read as too slow even after the Test 26 fix below:
  the component had been created before that fix, so the Inspector kept the
  original 1.5 regardless of what the script's default said.

--- Bonus enemy now "disappears" entirely when leftovers are cleared early ---
No code change here - just a clarification. The bonus enemy's vertical
overtake of the regular row (see bonusSpeedMultiplier, Test 38-40) is driven
by it reaching its OWN edge check roughly bonusSpeedMultiplier times as often
as the regular group, which means it also steps down that much more often.
If it isn't visibly passing the regular row by roughly the midpoint of a
typical wave, raise bonusSpeedMultiplier; if it passes almost immediately,
lower it. 1.8 is a starting point, not a verified-correct number.

--- Shield regression: destroyed shield doesn't come back on restart ---
Reported from actual play - a shield reduced to 0 hits never reappeared on
Restart (R), even though it used to. Cause: Shield.cs's OnTriggerEnter2D was
calling Destroy(gameObject) instead of gameObject.SetActive(false) once
hitsRemaining reached 0. The whole reset design (see Shield.cs's own class
note) depends on a depleted shield being DEACTIVATED, not destroyed -
GameManager.ResetGame()'s FindObjectsByType<Shield>(FindObjectsInactive.
Include) can only find and reset a GameObject that still exists, just
inactive. Destroying it removes it from the scene entirely, so there's
nothing left to find or reactivate.

@@ Shield.cs, OnTriggerEnter2D() @@
-        if (hitsRemaining <= 0) Destroy(gameObject);
+        if (hitsRemaining <= 0) gameObject.SetActive(false);

--- EnemySpawner.cs merge artifact: wave ending early / score jumping by 500 ---
Not a bug in this reference material - a hand-applied-diff artifact. If your
project's SpawnEnemies() has the bonus-enemy-spawning block INSIDE the
enemy-spawning for-loop (rather than once, after it), it fires once per
regular enemy instead of once per wave - 5 extra coroutine calls on Wave 2,
each spawning a duplicate bonus enemy at the exact same position, stacked
invisibly on top of each other. None of those duplicates are counted in
enemiesRemaining, so killing a few of the stack (which LOOKS like one gold
enemy) drains the counter to 0 early, ending the wave with real enemies
still on screen - and inflates the score by 50 points per extra duplicate
killed (500 points = all 10 of them). Compare your SpawnEnemies() against
the THIRD-PASS version above line by line - specifically, the whole
"if (hasBonusEnemy) { ...StartCoroutine... }" block must appear exactly
ONCE, after the for-loop's closing brace, not inside it.


